# Impeccable Design Guidelines for Apex Swap

This rule ensures that all generated frontends look like a human designer crafted them rather than an AI generator. Always apply these guidelines to visual styling.

## Typography Scale & Hierarchy
- **Header Font:** Use Plus Jakarta Sans or Figtree. Do not use default Inter or Arial.
- **Body Font:** Match with clean secondary font (line length capped between 65-75 characters).
- **Scale:**
  - Display / Hero (H1): 32px (used selectively, never for standard section headers)
  - Section Headers: 20px (clear visual landmarks)
  - Body Content: 16px (legible, warm contrast)
  - Secondary Elements: 14px (navigation, buttons, form labels)
  - Metadata / Badges: 12px (supporting data, status tags)

## OKLCH Color Axis & Commitment
- **No Pure Neutral Colors:** Never use raw `#000` or `#fff`. Use warm-tinted neutrals by adding 0.005 to 0.01 chroma tuned to the brand's primary hue.
- **Restrained Color Strategy (Product default):** Tinted neutrals dominate the UI, with a single high-saturation brand accent color representing $\le10\%$ of the screen (used only for primary CTAs).
- **Contrast:** Always verify WCAG AA compliance (4.5:1 contrast ratio). Never overlay gray text on saturated color backdrops.

## Absolute Design Bans (Match & Refuse)
- **No Side-Stripe Card Accents:** Do not use `border-left` or `border-right` greater than 1px as a colored accent on cards, lists, or alerts. Use standard borders or background tints.
- **No Gradient Text:** Strictly ban `background-clip: text` gradients on headers. Use solid, high-contrast, bold typography.
- **No Monotonous Card Grids:** Avoid repeated card layouts (icon + header + text in identical sizes). Use asymmetrical spacing, custom groupings, or left-aligned typography.
- **No Modals by Default:** Avoid popups or modals as a first resort. Exhaust inline or progressive disclosure patterns first.
- **No Bounce Transitions:** Do not use elastic or bouncy easing curves. Animations must use physics-based deceleration curves (`ease-out-quart` or `ease-out-expo`).
